        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: Size by Output, Not Square Feet
          </h2>

          <p>
            A humidifier is sized by how much water it can put into the air per day, usually listed in gallons per day or milliliters per hour. That output has to keep up with the moisture your room or house loses every hour as dry outdoor air leaks in and humid indoor air leaks out. The square-footage claim on the box is the manufacturer's estimate of where that output is enough, and it is almost always calculated for a tight, mild-weather room with the door shut.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a working rule for a single room with standard 8-foot ceilings, plan on roughly 1 to 1.5 gallons per day for a bedroom up to about 300 square feet, 2 to 3 gallons per day for a living room of 300 to 500 square feet, and 3 to 5 gallons per day for a large room or open area of 500 to 1,000 square feet. Past that point you are no longer shopping for a tabletop unit; you are looking at a console evaporative model or a furnace-mounted system.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those ranges are starting points, not answers. A drafty older house in Minnesota in January can need twice the output of a new, well-sealed house in Virginia for the same floor area. Ceiling height, open floor plans, wood stoves and forced-air heating all move the number. The rest of this guide shows how to adjust for each of those so you buy the right size the first time.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other half of the answer is that you should never size by output alone and walk away. A humidifier that is too small leaves the air dry, but one that is too large for a tight room will push humidity past 50 percent, fog the windows and feed mold on cold exterior walls. A cheap hygrometer, the kind covered in our <a href="/best-hygrometer/">hygrometer guide</a>, turns sizing from guesswork into a measurement you can check every morning.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Match daily output to daily moisture loss, then verify with a hygrometer.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Room size tells you where to start. Your house's air leakage and the outdoor temperature decide where you finish. If the hygrometer reads under 30 percent after two days of running on high, the unit is undersized for the space.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="reading-the-box" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Reading the Box: Gallons per Day, mL per Hour and Tank Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            Manufacturers list output in three different ways, and only one of them is directly useful. Gallons per day is the cleanest figure because it maps straight onto the sizing chart. Many ultrasonic and smaller units list milliliters per hour instead. To convert, multiply milliliters per hour by 24 and divide by 3,785. A unit rated at 300 mL per hour works out to about 1.9 gallons per day; 500 mL per hour is roughly 3.2 gallons per day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tank size is the number most shoppers confuse with output, and it is not the same thing at all. A 6-liter tank tells you how long the machine can run before you refill it, not how fast it humidifies. A small tank on a high-output machine empties quickly; a large tank on a low-output machine runs all night but may never raise the room's humidity. Always find the output figure first and treat the tank as a convenience number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run time is simply tank volume divided by output rate. A 1-gallon tank on a machine putting out 2 gallons per day runs for about 12 hours at full output. A 6-liter tank, which is about 1.6 gallons, on a machine rated at 300 mL per hour lasts roughly 20 hours on its highest setting. Most rated run times you see advertised are measured on the lowest setting, which is why they look so generous.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be cautious with output figures on evaporative humidifiers in particular. Their output depends on the dryness of the room, because a wick evaporates water faster in dry air than in humid air. A rating is typically measured under standardized test conditions, and real output will drop as the room approaches your target humidity. That is a useful self-limiting trait, but it means an evaporative unit needs a bit more rated headroom than an ultrasonic one to reach the same target in a very dry room.
          </p>

